# The Origin of the Northern Lights

Long ago, when the world was young, the Sky Fox lived among the stars, her fur shimmering with colors beyond mortal imagination. She was lonely, for she was the only creature who could dance across the heavens.

One winter night, the Sky Fox gazed down at Earth and saw children huddled around fires, their faces turned upward with wonder. Their eyes reflected starlight, and in that moment, she felt a connection to the world below.

The Sky Fox began to run across the frozen dome of the sky, her magnificent tail sweeping behind her in great arcs. Where her fur brushed against the darkness, it left trails of green, purple, and gold—the colors of her joy and longing.

The children pointed and laughed with delight, dancing and mimicking her movements on the snow below. The Sky Fox's heart swelled with happiness. She was no longer alone.

From that night forward, the Sky Fox runs across the northern sky whenever she feels the pull of connection with those below. The shimmering curtains of light are her gift—a reminder that even across the vast distance between earth and stars, wonder can bridge any divide.

The people call her dancing lights "aurora," meaning dawn, for they bring light to the darkest nights.
